{
  "abstract": "We prove two comparisons for the integral height calculation of unitary periods. On a marked two-dimensional arc we identify the horizontal Cartier boundary of the released theta model with that of the corresponding Lan chart. A three-valuation criterion detects vertical and residual discrepancies for other integral extensions. The same marked geometry identifies the odd projective theta metric and the Haar norm of the integral section, and a level-six grid recovers its stable scalar ideal. At a split word prime, simultaneous source characters in the actual theta moments yield extra divisibility through all $m$ lifts of the period. After gluing the same integral row, the equal-rank character decomposition gives a rank-preserving column enlargement of slope gain at least $(mf/2)\\log p$, where $f$ is the number of selected host places. The bottom quantile of the full rank-weighted digit law sharpens the centered frame inequality on that row, with a gain bounded below explicitly in terms of the retained rank and Gram errors. The application retains the signed height, spectral and uniform family comparisons. For the reference covolume on the fitted ordinary hosts, all surviving length-$t$ labels together cost at most the reference rank times $\\log|\\mathrm{Cl}(E)/(1+c)\\mathrm{Cl}(E)|$ per lift, where $c$ is complex conjugation on the host $E$. The remaining same-type index comes from labels without a length-$t$ part.",
